class.description:
[PXTGI3D] can't read a [ShaderMaterial]'s glow or colors, nor the colors of a [BaseMaterial3D]'s albedo texture: such a material needs a [code]pxtgi_emission[/code] and / or [code]pxtgi_albedo[/code] [Color] in its metadata for the light it gives off and bounces off screen. This measures what a material actually draws: each material on a tile of one HDR viewport, first with no lights and no environment light, at a known exposure, then under an even white ambient light, then in a white sky's reflections. The results include everything the shader does (textures, tints, any multiplier it hides), so they're the values the tags should have. The editor's [b]Measure Materials[/b] tool, on the [PXTGI3D] inspector, uses it.
Each material's glow is read several times over about half a second: it's the mean of those readings, and one that changes over time (animated by [code]TIME[/code]) is flagged. The glow is in the material's own units: nits with physical light units on. The albedo is the share of light the material bounces, read against white and black references drawn alongside: what the ambient light added (the diffuse color averaged over the surface, the material's [code]AO[/code] taken in), plus what the sky's reflections added past what a plain surface reflects. So a dielectric's albedo is its diffuse color, as [PXTGI3D] reads a [BaseMaterial3D]'s, and a metal's is its reflection as Godot draws it: about its color when glossy, around half of it at roughness 1. A material drawn [code]unshaded[/code] reflects nothing (it reads as all glow). Beside a very bright glow (thousands of times the albedo's scale) the albedo can't be told apart and reads less exactly.
[codeblock]
var meter := PXTGIEmissionMeter.new()
var found := PXTGIEmissionMeter.find_untagged(get_tree().edited_scene_root)
meter.measure(found.keys())
for result in await meter.finished:
    if result.luminance > 0.0:
        result.material.set_meta("pxtgi_emission", PXTGIEmissionMeter.emission_tag(result.glow))
[/codeblock]
